<!--
  Biira Bank lab, Wazuh manager custom ruleset.
  Deploys to SIEM01 at /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml

  Numbering: custom rules must be >= 100000. Blocks used here:
    100010-100019  Sysmon noise suppression
    100020-100029  Authorised scanner activity

  Two standing principles for everything in this file:

  1. Suppress at the alert layer, not the collection layer. The raw event
     still lands in the endpoint log and in the Wazuh archive. Only the
     alert is withheld. That keeps the forensic record intact, and it is
     reversible by deleting a rule instead of redeploying agent config.

  2. Never suppress on one field. Every exclusion below pins at least two
     independent conditions, so an attacker cannot walk into the exclusion
     just by copying a filename or a username.
-->

<group name="local,">

  <!--
    100010: PowerShell's own execution-policy probe.

    Parent 92213 ("Executable file dropped in folder commonly used by
    malware", level 15, T1105) fires on any script or binary written into a
    temp or profile folder. It is a sound heuristic and it is left enabled.

    PowerShell trips it constantly. Before running any script or loading any
    module it must resolve the execution policy, and it does that by writing
    a throwaway .ps1 into %TEMP%, submitting it to the policy provider,
    reading the verdict and deleting the file. Milliseconds, dozens of times
    an hour, on every Windows host.

    The filename is .NET Path.GetRandomFileName() output, which is always
    8.3 with lowercase alphanumerics, hence the tight pattern. Both the
    filename shape and the writing process must match, so a dropper merely
    named __PSScriptPolicyTest_something.ps1 still alerts at level 15.

    If this ever stops matching, loosen the {8} and {3} quantifiers first.
  -->
  <rule id="100010" level="0">
    <if_sid>92213</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\\Temp\\__PSScriptPolicyTest_[a-z0-9]{8}\.[a-z0-9]{3}\.ps1$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\powershell(_ise)?\.exe$</field>
    <description>Sysmon 11: PowerShell execution policy probe file. Expected, not alerted.</description>
  </rule>


  <!--
    100020 and 100021: the Greenbone credentialed scanner.

    Parent 92652 reports a successful network logon over NTLM and reads it as
    a possible pass-the-hash. During a credentialed scan svc-greenbone
    authenticates to every open service on the target, which produced 2051
    level 6 alerts from a single scan of one workstation.

    ORDER MATTERS. Wazuh evaluates sibling rules in file order and stops at
    the first match, so the narrow rule must come first. Reverse these two
    and every scanner logon alerts at level 12.

    The failure direction is deliberately safe: if ipAddress is absent or
    does not match SCAN01, 100020 misses and 100021 fires loudly. Silence is
    never the default.
  -->

  <!-- Expected: the scanner account, from SCAN01, and only from SCAN01. -->
  <rule id="100020" level="0">
    <if_sid>92652</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetUserName" type="pcre2">(?i)^svc-greenbone$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.ipAddress" type="pcre2">^192\.168\.20\.3$</field>
    <description>Authorised Greenbone credentialed scan logon from SCAN01. Expected, not alerted.</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- Anything else using that account is a real finding. It is a scanner
       service account: it has no interactive use and no reason to originate
       anywhere but SCAN01. -->
  <rule id="100021" level="12">
    <if_sid>92652</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetUserName" type="pcre2">(?i)^svc-greenbone$</field>
    <description>Scanner service account svc-greenbone authenticated from an unexpected source.</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078.002</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
